## Web3 & Blockchain

### Viem Best Practices
- Use `viem` for all Web3 interactions
- Prefer `publicClient` for read operations
- Use `walletClient` for write operations
- Always handle errors from blockchain calls
- Use proper types from `viem` (Address, Hash, etc.)

### Error Handling
```typescript
try {
  const hash = await walletClient.writeContract({
    address: contractAddress,
    abi: contractABI,
    functionName: 'stake',
    args: [tokenId]
  })
  
  const receipt = await publicClient.waitForTransactionReceipt({ hash })
  
  if (receipt.status === 'success') {
    // Handle success
  }
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof Error) {
    // Handle user rejection, insufficient funds, etc.
  }
}
```

---

## Supabase Best Practices

### Authentication
- Use Supabase Auth for user management
- Implement proper session handling
- Use Row Level Security (RLS) policies

### Database Queries
- Use TypeScript types from Supabase CLI
- Implement proper error handling
- Use `.select()` to specify needed columns only
- Use `.single()` when expecting one result

```typescript
const { data, error } = await supabase
  .from('users')
  .select('id, name, email')
  .eq('id', userId)
  .single()

if (error) {
  // Handle error
}
```

---

## Farcaster/Neynar Integration

### API Calls
- Use Neynar SDK for Farcaster interactions
- Cache responses when appropriate
- Handle rate limits gracefully
- Implement proper error boundaries

### Mini-App Specific
- Follow Farcaster Frames v2 specifications
- Implement proper meta tags for frame discovery
- Handle mobile and desktop experiences
- Test in Warpcast client

---

## Testing & Quality

### Before Committing
- Run `npm run lint` to check for linting errors
- Ensure TypeScript compiles without errors
- Test in both development and production builds
- Verify mobile responsiveness

### Code Review Checklist
- [ ] No console.logs in production code
- [ ] Proper error handling implemented
- [ ] Loading states handled
- [ ] Accessibility considerations (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation)
- [ ] Performance optimizations applied where needed
- [ ] Types are properly defined
- [ ] No unused imports or variables

---

## Security

### Environment Variables
- Never commit `.env` files
- Use `.env.example` for documentation
- Validate environment variables at startup
- Use different values for dev/staging/production

### API Keys & Secrets
- Store in environment variables
- Never expose in client-side code
- Use server-side API routes for sensitive operations
- Implement rate limiting on API routes

### Input Validation
- Validate all user inputs
- Sanitize data before database operations
- Use Zod or similar for runtime type checking
- Implement CSRF protection

---

## Git Workflow

### Commit Messages
- Use conventional commits format
- Examples:
  - `feat: add staking functionality`
  - `fix: resolve wallet connection issue`
  - `refactor: optimize component rendering`
  - `docs: update README with deployment steps`

### Branch Naming
- `feature/description` for new features
- `fix/description` for bug fixes
- `refactor/description` for refactoring
- `docs/description` for documentation

---

## Deployment

### Vercel
- Use `npm run deploy:vercel` for deployment
- Ensure environment variables are set in Vercel dashboard
- Test preview deployments before promoting to production
- Monitor performance and errors in Vercel Analytics

### Build Optimization
- Analyze bundle size with `npm run build`
- Check for unused dependencies
- Optimize images (use Next.js Image component)
- Implement proper caching headers

---

## Additional Notes

### When to Optimize
- Don't prematurely optimize
- Profile first, then optimize
- Focus on user-facing performance
- Measure impact of optimizations

### Documentation
- Document complex logic with comments
- Keep README up to date
- Document API endpoints
- Maintain changelog for significant changes

### Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML
- Implement keyboard navigation
- Add ARIA labels where needed
- Test with screen readers
- Ensure proper color contrast

